{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789845401,"nonce":"JljjREq02Wu3ER+uolKElKFh7f0=","message":"4097caf. Ceiling experiment first: with every GuardNumAt/GuardClassAt lowered to nothing (unsound, output still right) ash/nbody runs 0.586 s on the NUC — Ash's number — so the whole remaining gap was the dynamic checks, not the physics. Two sound ones landed: (1) every field-kind guard on a class tests all the fields the body guards on that class (kind_sets, collected from the MIR before lowering), so one unswitch strips them all — the seven per-field conditions had been hoisted to bools but LLVM will not version a loop 2^7 ways, so they were spilled and tested every iteration; (2) a GuardNumAt on a value already guarded in the same block is dropped (b.mass was CSE'd and guarded twice, the second by a NaN test). Idle NUC: ash/nbody 0.803 → 0.702, bench/nbody 0.105 → 0.092; the inner loop is 85 instructions, ~48 of them physics with memory operands. Left, in order of size: b's class check (~12 with the branchless select, its rematerialised tag constant and the register shuffles it causes) — removable only by tracking a List's element class the way a class tracks field kinds, i.e. every list write path notes the element's class (an audit of ObjList writers, same discipline as field kinds); a's class check (3; the hoisting pass does it but costs more in spills on x86-64); two safepoint polls where one would do; register pressure from the class-pointer and tag constants.","files":null}]}